# CA chain pinned for eserver.tulipapraha.com (Helios B2B endpoint).
# The server presents a RapidSSL (DigiCert) certificate for *.tulipapraha.com but does
# not send the intermediate, so default verification fails ("unable to get local issuer
# certificate"). Instead of verify=False, HeliosClient trusts exactly this chain:
#   1. RapidSSL TLS RSA CA G1 (intermediate, valid until 2027-11-02)
#      SHA-256 44:22:E9:63:EE:53:CD:58:CC:9F:85:CD:40:BF:5F:FE:C0:09:5F:DF:1A:15:45:35:66:1C:1C:06:BC:AD:C6:9B
#   2. DigiCert Global Root G2 (root, valid until 2038-01-15)
#      SHA-256 CB:3C:CB:B7:60:31:E5:E0:13:8F:8D:D3:9A:23:F9:DE:47:FF:C3:5E:43:C1:14:4C:EA:27:D4:6A:5A:B1:CB:5F
# Server certificate when pinned (2026-10-07): CN=*.tulipapraha.com, valid until 2027-02-13,
#      SHA-256 2E:0C:66:8A:A3:1E:BD:B5:0E:90:F9:91:00:5D:A1:B6:D4:E5:16:DC:B0:24:E0:60:3B:11:A5:45:D8:17:54:91
# If Tulipa renews onto another intermediate, login fails with an SSL error: rebuild this
# file from `openssl s_client -connect eserver.tulipapraha.com:4343 -showcerts` and the
# leaf's "CA Issuers" URL, and check the fingerprints out of band.
